Some capabilities are inherently application-dependent. GTK/AT-SPI may expose rich controls in one application while another may provide only a window or no useful tree. Apps without semantic access need visual targeting, browser-specific BAS control, terminal control, or an application-specific integration. Launching or installing an arbitrary desktop application is also a separate capability; screenshot access alone does not provide it.

For the whole “Portal everywhere” product, additional gates remain:

- live GNOME and KDE Wayland sessions with working Screenshot and RemoteDesktop portals;
- live Windows UI Automation and macOS Accessibility/ScreenCaptureKit journeys;
- signed, installable, recoverable native companion/helper packages;
- remote Bridge sessions, streaming, input-channel revocation, and reconnect behavior;
- optional-provider routing with clear missing/denied/unsupported states;
- full regression and performance evidence.

The acceptance ledger currently stands at **89/93 cases**, **1/5 platform rows**, and **14/16 deliverables**. The four missing cases are exactly the Windows, macOS, GNOME Wayland, and KDE Wayland gates; the Linux X11 capture path is the part already proven here.

